  "qualification": [
    "All successful native after-file bytes agree between the two captures. parse-junk-size is excluded from a successful Rust contract because the source reads an uninitialized variable.",
    "Custom alignment may preserve a metadata alignment that disagrees with the source tool data padding, and canonicalized zero-dimensional/zero-extent tensors may produce original-invalid files. Successful output bytes must be preserved without asking the stricter reader to accept them.",
    "NUL-containing metadata strings are truncated according to original C-string transfer. Integer tensor limits retain atoi-prefix behavior; negative divisors work, and zero limits succeed when zero/one tensor short-circuits the modulo operation.",
    "Split dry-run reads headers and can succeed on truncated payloads. Merge dry-run still reads every payload and rejects the captured truncated secondary.",
    "The source advertises -h but its parser enters option handling only for -- prefixes; captured -h is a normal rejected argument.",
    "Version/build-banner bytes identify the original build and are retained only as evidence; Rust must identify its own build.",
    "Delete-splits in Rust is deliberately strengthened to delete only after the completed output is flushed/synced. Successful bytes and final deletions match the source; deletion log order and mid-failure destructive behavior are not replayed.",
    "Failed truncated-output bytes differ between original compilers. Their complete bytes are retained but the Rust adapter requires a checked error and intact inputs rather than failed partial-output byte parity.",
    "The no-deletion guarantee applies to failures before that complete output has been flushed and synced. Once cleanup starts, removal or logging can fail after some shards have already been deleted; the completed output remains. Destructive cleanup requires stable, exclusively managed input and output paths. Safe standard Rust does not provide an atomic file-identity-check-and-unlink operation, so arbitrary concurrent path replacement is outside this cleanup guarantee. The truncated-secondary regression exercises only failure before cleanup, not a rollback guarantee for cleanup errors."
  ],
